: CREATE-FILE ( c-addr u mode --- fid ior)
\ Create a new file with the name starting at c-addr with length u.
\ Return the file-ID and the IO result. (ior=0 if success)
\ R/W (with BIN or not) is the engine's mode 8 or 9 - created, read and
\ written; the others as before, created to write. It was 1 AND, and a file
\ created R/W could not be read (the standard suite's filetest; Iteration 168).
  DUP 4 AND IF 4 + ELSE 1 AND THEN OPEN-FILE ;

\ : READ-LINE >R OVER R> READ-FILE >R OVER + SWAP 
\ BEGIN WHILE REPEAT 
\ R> ;

\ PART 7: SOURCE INPUT WORDS.

VARIABLE ACC-W ( --- addr)
\ ACCEPT: the characters kept so far.

: ACC-BYTE ( c-addr n1 c --- c-addr n1 f )
\ One character as ACCEPT always took it, ACC-W the characters kept so
\ far: backspace and DEL remove the previous one, LF and CR end the
\ line (f true), and characters past n1 are dropped.
  DUP 8 = OVER 127 = OR IF DROP ACC-W @ IF -1 ACC-W +! THEN 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP 10 = OVER 13 = OR IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  OVER ACC-W @ - IF >R OVER ACC-W @ + R> SWAP C! 1 ACC-W +! ELSE DROP THEN 0 ;

: ACCEPT ( c-addr n1 --- n2 )
\ Read a line from the terminal to a buffer starting at c-addr with
\ length n1. n2 is the number of characters read. One READ a line, not
\ a KEY a character: READ stops after the first LF, CR, backspace or
\ DEL, so of what lands in the buffer only the LAST byte can be one of
\ them - the rest are characters already in place, counted at once, and
\ ACC-BYTE takes the last as the character loop took every byte. With
\ the buffer full, a byte at a time: only a backspace can make room. At
\ the end of input, leave as BYE does, as KEY does.
  0 ACC-W !
  BEGIN
    OVER ACC-W @ +  OVER ACC-W @ -
    DUP 0= IF 2DROP KEY-BYTE 1 THEN
    OVER SWAP READ  DUP 0= IF BYE THEN
    DUP 1- ACC-W +!  + 1- C@
    ACC-BYTE IF 2DROP ACC-W @ EXIT THEN
  0 UNTIL ;

VARIABLE TIB ( --- addr)
\ is the standard terminal input buffer.
256 CHARS-T ALLOT-T

VARIABLE SPAN ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds the number of characters read by EXPECT.

VARIABLE #TIB ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds the number of characters in the terminal input buffer.

VARIABLE >IN  ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds an index in the current input source where the next word
\ will be parsed.

VARIABLE SID  ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds the source i.d. returned by SOURCE-ID.

VARIABLE SRC  ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds the address of the current input source.

VARIABLE #SRC ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds the length of the current input source.

: EXPECT ( c-addr u --- )
\ Read a line from the terminal to a buffer at c-addr with length u.
\ Store the length of the line in SPAN.
  ACCEPT SPAN ! ;

: QUERY ( --- )
\ Read a line from the terminal into the terminal input buffer.
  TIB 256 ACCEPT #TIB ! ;

: SOURCE ( --- addr len)
\ Return the address and length of the current input source.
   SRC @ #SRC @ ;

: SOURCE-ID ( --- sid)
\ Return the i.d. of the current source i.d., 0 for terminal, -1
\ for EVALUATE and positive number for INCLUDE file.
   SID @ ;

: REFILL ( --- f)
\ Refill the current input source when it is exhausted. f is
\ true if it was successfully refilled.
  SOURCE-ID -1 = IF
   0 \ Not refillable for EVALUATE
  ELSE
   SOURCE-ID IF
    SRC @ 256 SOURCE-ID READ-LINE ABORT" Error reading file"
    SWAP #SRC ! 0 >IN !
    \ No tab is made a space: SKIP and SCAN take any control character for a
    \ space delimiter (Iteration 145).
    \ flag from READ-LINE is returned (no success at EOF)
   ELSE
    QUERY #TIB @ #SRC ! 0 >IN ! -1 \ Always successful from terminal.
   THEN
  THEN
;

: SCAN ( c-addr1 u1 c --- c-addr2 u2 )
\ Find the first occurrence of character c in the string c-addr1 u1
\ c-addr2 u2 is the remaining part of the string starting with that char.
\ It is a zero-length string if c was not found.
\ As SKIP: 1 -1 D+ to move on; a space delimiter is met by any control
\ character too.
  DUP BL = IF DROP BEGIN DUP WHILE OVER C@ 33 U< 0= WHILE 1 -1 D+ REPEAT THEN EXIT THEN
  >R BEGIN DUP WHILE OVER C@ R@ = 0= WHILE 1 -1 D+ REPEAT THEN R> DROP
;

: SKIP ( c-addr1 u1 c --- c-addr2 u2 )
\ Find the first character not equal to c in the string c-addr1 u1
\ c-addr2 u2 is the remaining part of the string starting with the
\ nonmatching char. It is a zero-length string if no other chars found.
\ The delimiter on the return stack, the string moved on by 1 -1 D+ - one
\ double add for the address up and the length down; a space delimiter
\ skips every control character too, as the standard allows (3.4.1.1) - so
\ no tab need ever be made a space (the audit, Iteration 145).
  DUP BL = IF DROP BEGIN DUP WHILE OVER C@ 33 U< WHILE 1 -1 D+ REPEAT THEN EXIT THEN
  >R BEGIN DUP WHILE OVER C@ R@ = WHILE 1 -1 D+ REPEAT THEN R> DROP
;

: PARSE-WORD ( c --- addr len )
\ WORD's parse: skip the leading c's, then the character sequence
\ delimited by c; >IN 1 past the end delimiter. This was PARSE, which
\ skipped them as SOD32's does and the standard's does not - S" " took
\ its closing quote for a leading delimiter and swallowed the rest of the
\ line (Iteration 125). WORD keeps the skip, and its speed.
  >R SOURCE >IN @ - SWAP >IN @ + R> OVER >R >R SWAP
  R@ SKIP OVER R> SWAP >R SCAN IF 1 >IN +! THEN
  DUP R@ - R> SWAP
  ROT R> - >IN +! ;

: PARSE ( c --- c-addr u )
\ Parse ccc delimited by c from the input source and step past the
\ delimiter. Leading delimiters are NOT skipped - that is WORD's job, and
\ the standard's (Iteration 125, RelF's Iteration 245).
  >R SOURCE SWAP >IN @ + SWAP >IN @ -   ( a n )
  OVER SWAP R> SCAN                     ( a a2 n2 )
  IF 1 >IN +! THEN
  OVER - DUP >IN +! ;

: PLACE ( addr len c-addr --- )
\ Place the string starting at addr with length len at c-addr as
\ a counted string.
  2DUP C! CHAR+ SWAP CMOVE ;

: WORD ( c --- addr )
\ Parse a character sequence delimited by character c and return the
\ address of a counted string that is a copy of it. The counted
\ string is actually placed at HERE. The character after the counted
\ string is set to a space.
  PARSE-WORD HERE DUP >R PLACE BL R@ COUNT + C! R> ;   \ HERE once, as klookup's WORD8

\ PART 8: INTERPRETER HELPER WORDS

\ First we need FIND and related words.

\ Each word list consists of one linked list of definitions. No hashing
\ is used to speed up dictionary search. All names in the dictionary
\ are at aligned addresses and FIND is optimized to compare one 4-byte
\ cell at a time.

\ Dictionary definitions are built as follows:
\
\ LINK field: 1 cell, aligned, contains name field of previous word in list.
\ NAME field: counted string of at most 31 characters.
\             bits 5-7 of length byte have special meaning.
\                   7 is always set to mark start of name ( for >NAME)
\                   6 is set if the word is immediate.
\                   5 is set if the word is a macro.
\ CODE field: first aligned address after name, is execution token for word.
\             here the executable code for the word starts. (is 1 cell for
\             variables etc.)
\ PARAMETER field: (body) Contains the data of constants and variables etc.

VARIABLE NAMEBUF ( --- a-addr)
\ An aligned buffer that holds a copy of the name that is searched - 32
\ bytes and the cell after them, which the search compares to find the
\ name's end (the cell after a 31-character name was past it: the audit).
32 ALLOT-T

VARIABLE FORTH-WORDLIST ( --- addr)
\ A hashed word list, laid out as SOD32's is: cell 0 holds the number
\ of threads and cells 1..n the head of each thread.
\
\ RelF carried this structure over from SOD32 as a SINGLE cell - "a
\ pointer to the last definition" - which made every lookup a linear
\ scan of the whole dictionary. Measured on bench/parse.fth that cost
\ 8.05 million dictionary entries visited against SOD32's 397 thousand,
\ and about 81% of every operation the system executed. See
\ FINDINGS-OUTER-INTERPRETER.md.
32 CELLS-T ALLOT-T

VARIABLE LAST ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds a pointer to the last definition created.

VARIABLE CONTEXT 28 ALLOT-T ( --- a-addr)
\ This variable holds the addresses of up to 8 word lists that are
\ in the search order.

VARIABLE #ORDER ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds the number of word list that are in the search order.

VARIABLE CURRENT ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds the address of the word list to which new definitions
\ are added.

: HASH ( c-addr u #threads --- n )
\ SOD32's hash, unchanged: the length and the first two characters.
\ It must agree EXACTLY with cross.4's THASH, which threads the
\ dictionary of the image being built, or a word would be filed under
\ one thread and looked for under another.
  >R OVER C@ 1 LSHIFT OVER 1 > IF ROT 1+ C@ 2 LSHIFT XOR ELSE ROT DROP
   THEN XOR
  R> 1- AND ;

: NAME>BUF ( c-addr u ---)
\ Move the name c-addr u to the aligned buffer NAMEBUF. The search compares
\ cells, so the name's last cell is zeroed for its padding and the one after
\ for the compare's end - not 32 bytes a byte at a time (the audit).
  32 MIN DUP 1+ ALIGNED NAMEBUF +  0 OVER !  0 SWAP 1 CELLS - !  NAMEBUF PLACE ;

: SEARCH-WORDLIST ( c-addr u wid --- 0 | xt 1 xt -1)
\ Search the wordlist with address wid for the name c-addr u.
\ Return 0 if not found, the execution token xt and -1 for non-immediate
\ words and xt and 1 for immediate words.
  ROT ROT
  NAME>BUF
  \ Pick the thread this name would have been filed under. Everything
  \ below is unchanged: the chain walk, the cell-at-a-time compare and
  \ the relative link field are all as they were.
  DUP >R NAMEBUF COUNT R@ @ HASH 1+ CELLS + @ R> DROP
  DUP IF
  BEGIN
   \ Mask clears the flag bits within the count byte - which, since cells
   \ are read native-endian (little-endian on every host targeted so
   \ far - see GOALS.md phase 5), is the LOW byte of the cell, not the
   \ high byte the old big-endian-packed mask (1 61 LSHIFT 1 -) assumed.
   NAMEBUF @ OVER @ -225 AND = \ Compare first cells of names (-225: -1 224 XOR, once).
           \ Mask off bits 5-7 of name length, these have special purposes.
   IF
    NAMEBUF CELL+
    OVER CELL+
    CELLBYTES-TOK >R
    BEGIN
     OVER @ OVER @ - >R
     CELL+ SWAP CELL+ SWAP R> R> CELL+ >R \ Compare all of name until different
    UNTIL
    R> NAMEBUF C@ 1 + ALIGNED CELL+ = IF \ match to the right length then found
     SWAP DROP SWAP C@
     64 AND IF \ determine immediateness of a word from bit 6 of name length.
      1
     ELSE
      -1
     THEN
     SWAP CELLBYTES-TOK NEGATE + SWAP EXIT \ Exit with execution token and flag.
    THEN
    2DROP
   THEN
   CELLBYTES-TOK NEGATE + DUP @ DUP ROT ROT + SWAP 0 =  \ Take address of next word from link field until 0
  UNTIL
  THEN
  DROP 0 \ Not found.
;

: FIND ( c-addr --- c-addr 0| xt 1|xt -1 )
\ Search all word lists in the search order for the name in the
\ counted string at c-addr. If not found return the name address and 0.
\ If found return the execution token xt and -1 if the word is non-immediate
\ and 1 if the word is immediate.
  #ORDER @ DUP 1 > IF
   CONTEXT #ORDER @ 1- CELLS + DUP @ SWAP CELLBYTES-TOK - @ =
  ELSE 0 THEN
  IF 1- THEN \ If last wordlist is double, don't search it twice.
  BEGIN
   DUP
  WHILE
   1- >R
   DUP COUNT
   R@ CELLS CONTEXT + @ SEARCH-WORDLIST
   DUP
   IF
    R> DROP ROT DROP EXIT \ Exit if found.
   THEN
   DROP R>
  REPEAT
;

\ The following words are related to numeric input.

: DIGIT? ( c -- 0| c--- n -1)
\ Convert character c to its digit value n and return true if c is a
\ digit in the current base. Otherwise return false.
  48 - DUP 0< IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP 9 > OVER 17 < AND IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP 9 > IF 7 - THEN
  DUP BASE @ < 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  -1
;

: >NUMBER ( ud1 c-addr1 u1 --- ud2 c-addr2 u2 )
\ Convert the string at c-addr with length u1 to binary, multiplying ud1
\ by the number in BASE and adding the digit value to it for each digit.
\ c-addr2 u2 is the remainder of the string starting at the first character
\ that is no digit.
  BEGIN
   DUP
  WHILE
   -1 + >R
   COUNT DIGIT? 0=
   IF
    R> 1+ SWAP -1 + SWAP  EXIT
   THEN
   SWAP >R
   >R
   SWAP BASE @ UM* ROT BASE @ * 0 SWAP D+ \ Multiply ud by base.
   R> 0 D+                                \ Add new digit.
   R> R>
  REPEAT
;

: CONVERT ( ud1 c-addr1 --- ud2 c-addr2)
\ Convert the string starting at c-addr1 + 1 to binary. c-addr2 is the
\ address of the first non-digit. Digits are added into ud1 as in >NUMBER
\ It began at c-addr1 - 1 (`-1 +` for `1+`) and converted nothing
\ (Iteration 125; RelF's Iteration 554).
  1+ -1 >NUMBER DROP ;

: NUMBER? ( c-addr ---- d f)
\ Convert the counted string at c-addr to a double binary number.
\ f is true if and only if the conversion was successful. DPL contains
\ -1 if there was no point in the number, else the position of the point
\ from the right. Special prefixes: # decimal, $ hex, % binary, and 'c' a
\ character (Forth 2012, 3.4.1.3) - the sign after the prefix as the
\ standard writes it (#-1289), or before it as this kernel always took it
\ (Iteration 167).
  DUP C@ 3 = IF DUP 1+ C@ 39 = OVER 3 + C@ 39 = AND IF -1 DPL ! 2 + C@ 0 -1 EXIT THEN THEN
  -1 DPL !
  BASE @ >R
  COUNT
  OVER C@ 45 = DUP >R IF    -1 + SWAP 1 + SWAP THEN \ Get any - sign
  OVER C@ 36 = IF 16 BASE ! -1 + SWAP 1 + SWAP THEN   \ $ sign for hex.
  OVER C@ 35 = IF 10 BASE ! -1 + SWAP 1 + SWAP THEN   \ # sign for decimal
  OVER C@ 37 = IF  2 BASE ! -1 + SWAP 1 + SWAP THEN   \ % for binary
  R@ 0= IF OVER C@ 45 = IF R> DROP -1 >R -1 + SWAP 1 + SWAP THEN THEN   \ a sign after the prefix
  DUP  0 > 0= IF  R> DROP R> BASE ! 0 EXIT THEN   \ Length 0 or less?
  >R >R 0 0 R> R>
  BEGIN
   >NUMBER
   DUP IF OVER C@ 46 = IF -1 + DUP DPL ! SWAP 1 + SWAP ELSE \ handle point.
         R> DROP R> BASE ! 0 EXIT THEN   \ Error if anything but point
       THEN
  DUP 0= UNTIL 2DROP R> IF DNEGATE THEN
  R> BASE ! -1
;

\ PART 9: THE COMPILER

VARIABLE HANDLER ( --- a-addr)
\ The newest CATCH frame on the return stack, or 0 (Forth 2012, 9.6; the
\ standard's reference design). ABORT is -1 THROW, ABORT" -2 THROW, an
\ undefined word -13 THROW under a CATCH; with none, THROW does what ABORT
\ always did - the same messages, then WARM (Iteration 171). Any other code
\ uncaught is named first: "Division by zero" for -10, which UM/MOD throws
\ for a zero divisor (the engine, Iteration 229), "Error n" for the rest.
\ The frame keeps the input source too: THROW restores the one in use when
\ CATCH began, as the standard has it - an EVALUATE's own restoring is on the
\ return stack THROW cuts back (exceptiontest's -13 in three EVALUATEs).
: CATCH ( i*x xt --- j*x 0 | i*x n)
  SP@ >R  SRC @ >R #SRC @ >R >IN @ >R SID @ >R  HANDLER @ >R  RP@ HANDLER !
  EXECUTE  R> HANDLER !  R> DROP R> DROP R> DROP R> DROP  R> DROP  0 ;
: THROW ( k*x n --- k*x | i*x n)
  ?DUP IF HANDLER @ ?DUP IF RP! R> HANDLER !  R> SID ! R> >IN ! R> #SRC ! R> SRC !
          R> SWAP >R SP! DROP R> EXIT THEN
      DUP 2 + 2 U< 0= IF DUP -10 = IF ." Division by zero" ELSE ." Error " DUP . THEN CR THEN
      WARM THEN ;
: (ABORT") ( f --- )
\ Runtime part of ABORT"
           IF HANDLER @ IF -2 THROW THEN R> COUNT TYPE SPACE HERE COUNT TYPE CR -2 THROW
           ELSE R> COUNT + ALIGNED >R THEN ;


VARIABLE DICT-MAX ( --- a-addr)
\ The dictionary's room in bytes from START where the engine keeps the stacks
\ apart (the converter writes it); 0 where they sit above the dictionary - its
\ limit then S0 less a margin, as always (Iteration 184).
: DICT-LIMIT ( --- addr)
  DICT-MAX @ ?DUP IF START @ + 4096 - ELSE S0 @ 10000 CELLS - 4096 - THEN ;
: ALLOT ( n --- )
\ Allot n extra bytes of memory, starting at HERE to the dictionary.
\ Refused past the data stack's room - the 10000 cells ?STACK allows below
\ S0, and a page: a big ALLOT jumped over every guard and wrote past the
\ engine's memory - a crash (Iteration 161; gdb: a byte store past the
\ end), where RelF's own kernel refuses. Each header ends with
\ ALLOT, so a definition is checked once.
\ n against the room left, not HERE + n against the limit: with 32-bit cells a
\ dictionary placed high wrapped HERE + n under the limit (Iteration 184).
  DUP 0> IF DUP DICT-LIMIT HERE - U> IF -1 ABORT" dictionary full" THEN THEN DP +! ;   \ n < 0 gives room back: unchecked

: , ( x --- )
\ Append cell x to the dictionary at HERE - DP +!, not ALLOT: the room is
\ checked once per definition, not per cell (Iteration 161).
  HERE ! CELLBYTES-TOK DP +! ;

: C, ( n --- )
\ Append character c to the dictionary at HERE - DP +!, as , (Iteration 161).
  HERE C! 1 DP +! ;

: ALIGN ( --- )
\ Add as many bytes to the dictionary as needed to align dictionary pointer.
  BEGIN HERE CELLBYTES-TOK 1- AND WHILE 0 C, REPEAT ;

: >NAME ( addr1 --- addr2 )
\ Convert execution token addr1 (address of code) to address of name.
  BEGIN 1- DUP C@ 128 AND UNTIL ;

: NAME> ( addr1 --- addr2 )
\ Convert address of name to address of code.
  COUNT 31 AND + ALIGNED ;

: HEADER ( --- )
\ Create a header for a new definition without a code field.
  ALIGN
  \ The link field cannot be filled in yet: which thread this word
  \ belongs to depends on its name, and the name has not been parsed.
  \ So reserve the cell, remember where it is, and patch it below -
  \ which is what SOD32 does.
  HERE >R 0 ,
  HERE LAST !         \ Set LAST so definition can be linked by REVEAL
  32 WORD DUP C@ 31 > ABORT" name too long" DUP FIND IF ." Redefining: " HERE COUNT TYPE CR THEN DROP
                       \ Give warning if existing word redefined.
  DUP COUNT CURRENT @ @ HASH 1+ CELLS CURRENT @ + @
  ?DUP IF R@ - R@ ! THEN    \ link, relative to the link cell itself
  R> DROP
  C@ 1+ HERE C@ 128 + HERE C! ALLOT ALIGN
                       \ Allot the name and set bit 7 in length byte.
;

: REVEAL ( --- )
\ Add the last created definition to the CURRENT wordlist, at the head
\ of the thread its name hashes to.
  LAST @ DUP COUNT 31 AND CURRENT @ @ HASH
  1+ CELLS CURRENT @ + ! ;

: CREATE ( "ccc" --- )
\ Create a definition that returns its parameter field address when
\ executed. Storage can be added to it with ALLOT.
  HEADER REVEAL POSTPONE DOVAR ;

: VARIABLE ( "ccc" --- )
\ Create a variable where 1 cell can be stored. When executed it
\ returns the address.
  CREATE 0 , ;

: CONSTANT ( x "ccc" ---)
\ Create a definition that returns x when executed.
\ Definition contains lit & return in its code field.
  HEADER REVEAL LIT-TOK , , EXIT-TOK , ;


VARIABLE STATE ( --- a-addr)
\ Variable that holds the compiler state, 0 is interpreting 1 is compiling.

: ]  ( --- )
\ Start compilation mode.
  1 STATE ! ;

: [  ( --- )
\ Leave compilation mode.
  0 STATE ! ; IMMEDIATE

: LITERAL ( n --- )
\ Add a literal to the current definition.
  LIT-TOK , , ; IMMEDIATE

: COMPILE, ( xt --- )
\ Add the execution semantics of the definition xt to the current definition.
  DUP >NAME C@ 32 AND \ if primitive compile inline
  IF @ , ELSE HERE - CELL- , THEN
;

VARIABLE CSP ( --- a-addr )
\ This variable is used for stack checking between : and ;

VARIABLE 'LEAVE ( --- a-addr)
\ This variable is used for LEAVE address resolution.

: !CSP ( --- )
\ Store current stack pointer in CSP.
   SP@ CSP ! ;

: ?CSP ( --- )
\ Check that stack pointer is equal to value contained in CSP.
   SP@ CSP @ - ABORT" Incomplete control structure" ;

: ?COMP ( --- )
\ Refuse a word that lays down code outside a definition (Iteration 125,
\ RelF's Iteration 552): interpreted, IF and its kind wrote branches at
\ HERE, and an IF at the top level killed the engine.
  STATE @ 0= ABORT" compile only" ;

: ; ( --- )
\ Finish the current definition by adding a return to it, make it
\ visible and leave compilation mode.
  ?COMP EXIT-TOK , [ ?CSP REVEAL ; IMMEDIATE

: (POSTPONE) ( --- )
\ Runtime for POSTPONE.
\ has inline argument.
  R> DUP DUP @ + SWAP CELL+ >R
  DUP >NAME C@ 64 AND IF EXECUTE ELSE COMPILE, THEN
;

: : ( "ccc" --- )
\ Start a new definition, enter compilation mode.
  !CSP HEADER ] ;

: BEGIN ( --- x )
\ Start a BEGIN UNTIL or BEGIN WHILE REPEAT loop.
  ?COMP HERE ; IMMEDIATE

: UNTIL ( x --- )
\ Form a loop with matching BEGIN.
\ Runtime: A flag is take from the stack
\ each time UNTIL is encountered and the loop iterates until it is nonzero.
  ?COMP 0BRANCH-TOK , HERE - , ; IMMEDIATE

: IF    ( --- x)
\ Start an IF THEN or IF ELSE THEN construction.
\ Runtime: At IF a flag is taken from
\ the stack and if it is true the part between IF and ELSE is executed,
\ otherwise the part between ELSE and THEN. If there is no ELSE, the part
\ between IF and THEN is executed only if flag is true.
  ?COMP 0BRANCH-TOK , HERE 1 CELLS ALLOT ; IMMEDIATE

: THEN ( x ---)
\ End an IF THEN or IF ELSE THEN construction.
  ?COMP HERE OVER - SWAP ! ; IMMEDIATE

: ELSE ( x1 --- x2)
\ part of IF ELSE THEN construction.
  ?COMP BRANCH-TOK , HERE 1 CELLS ALLOT SWAP HERE OVER - SWAP ! ; IMMEDIATE

: WHILE  ( x1 --- x2 x1 )
\ part of BEGIN WHILE REPEAT construction.
\ Runtime: At WHILE a flag is taken from the stack. If it is false,
\  the program jumps out of the loop, otherwise the part between WHILE
\  and REPEAT is executed and the loop iterates to BEGIN.
  ?COMP POSTPONE IF SWAP ; IMMEDIATE

: REPEAT ( x1 x2 --- )
\ part of BEGIN WHILE REPEAT construction.
  ?COMP BRANCH-TOK , HERE - , HERE OVER - SWAP ! ; IMMEDIATE

VARIABLE POCKET ( --- a-addr )
\ Buffer for S" strings that are interpreted.
  252 ALLOT-T

: '  ( "ccc" --- xt)
\ Find the word with name ccc and return its execution token.
  32 WORD FIND 0= ABORT" Not found" ;

: ['] ( "ccc" ---)
\ Compile the execution token of the word with name ccc as a literal.
  ' LITERAL ; IMMEDIATE

: CHAR ( "ccc" --- c)
\ Return the first character of "ccc".
  BL WORD 1 + C@ ;

: [CHAR] ( "ccc" --- )
\ Compile the first character of "ccc" as a literal.
  CHAR LITERAL ; IMMEDIATE

: DO ( --- x)
\ Start a DO LOOP.
\ Runtime: ( n1 n2 --- ) start a loop with initial count n2 and
\ limit n1.
  ?COMP POSTPONE (DO) 'LEAVE @ HERE 0 'LEAVE ! ; IMMEDIATE

: ?DO ( --- x )
\ Start a ?DO LOOP.
\ Runtime: ( n1 n2 --- ) start a loop with initial count n2 and
\ limit n1. Exit immediately if n1 = n2.
  ?COMP POSTPONE (?DO) 'LEAVE @ HERE 'LEAVE ! 0 , HERE ; IMMEDIATE

: LEAVE ( --- )
\ Runtime: leave the matching DO LOOP immediately.
\ All places where a leave address for the loop is needed are in a linked
\ list, starting with 'LEAVE variable, the other links in the cells where
\ the leave addresses will come.
  ?COMP POSTPONE (LEAVE) HERE 'LEAVE @ , 'LEAVE ! ; IMMEDIATE

: RESOLVE-LEAVE
\ Resolve the references to the leave addresses of the loop.
\ Each leave address is stored as an OFFSET from the cell that holds it,
\ as every other branch in this system is. It was an absolute address -
\ correct in the session that compiled it and wrong in any image saved
\ and loaded elsewhere, where a skipped ?DO or a LEAVE jumped into the
\ old address space. (?DO) and (LEAVE) add the offset back.
          'LEAVE @
          BEGIN DUP WHILE DUP @ HERE 2 PICK - ROT ! REPEAT DROP ;

: LOOP  ( x --- )
\ End a DO LOOP.
\ Runtime: Add 1 to the count and if it is equal to the limit leave the loop.
  ?COMP POSTPONE (LOOP) HERE - , RESOLVE-LEAVE 'LEAVE ! ; IMMEDIATE

: +LOOP ( x --- )
\ End a DO +LOOP
\ Runtime: ( n ---) Add n to the count and exit if this crosses the
\ boundary between limit-1 and limit.
  ?COMP POSTPONE (+LOOP) HERE - , RESOLVE-LEAVE 'LEAVE ! ; IMMEDIATE

: RECURSE ( --- )
\ Compile a call to the current (not yet finished) definition.
  ?COMP LAST @ NAME> COMPILE, ; IMMEDIATE

: STRING, ( c-addr u --- )
\ Compile a counted string inline and align - what (S"), (.") and
\ (ABORT") skip at run time (Iteration 125; RelF's, aligned here).
  DUP C, HERE OVER ALLOT SWAP MOVE ALIGN ;

: ."  ( "ccc<quote>" --- )
\ Parse a string delimited by " and compile the following runtime semantics.
\ Runtime: type that string.
   POSTPONE (.") 34 PARSE STRING, ; IMMEDIATE


: S"  ( "ccc<quote>" --- )
\ Parse a string delimited by " and compile the following runtime semantics.
\ Runtime: ( --- c-addr u) Return start address and length of that string.
  STATE @ IF POSTPONE (S") 34 PARSE STRING,
             ELSE 34 PARSE POCKET PLACE POCKET COUNT THEN ; IMMEDIATE

: ABORT"  ( "ccc<quote>" --- )
\ Parse a string delimited by " and compile the following runtime semantics.
\ Runtime: ( f --- ) if f is nonzero, print the string and abort program.
\ Compiled as IF -1 (ABORT") "ccc" THEN: a false flag branches past the call
\ and its string - one operation, where (ABORT") was called to test it. The
\ gene abortif guards the kernel's own sites the same way in the converter,
\ which never sees what is compiled at run time (the audit, Iteration 157).
  POSTPONE IF -1 POSTPONE LITERAL POSTPONE (ABORT") 34 PARSE STRING, POSTPONE THEN ; IMMEDIATE

: ABORT ( --- )
\ Abort unconditionally without a message: WARM, as (ABORT") does after
\ its message. It was `1 ABORT" "`, whose empty string the cross
\ compiler's WORD read to the end of the line, so ABORT printed " ;" and
\ the last word read (Iteration 125; RelF made it -1 THROW).
  -1 THROW ;

: POSTPONE ( "ccc" --- )
\ Parse the next word delimited by spaces and compile the following runtime.
\ Runtime: depending on immediateness EXECUTE or compile the execution
\ semantics of the parsed word.
  POSTPONE (POSTPONE) ' HERE - , ; IMMEDIATE

: IMMEDIATE ( --- )
\ Make last definition immediate, so that it will be executed even in
\ compilation mode.
  LAST @ DUP C@ 64 OR SWAP C! ;

: ( ( "ccc<rparen>" --- )
\ Comment till next ).
  BEGIN
    41 DUP PARSE + C@ = 0=
\    [CHAR] ) DUP PARSE + C@ = 0=
  WHILE
    REFILL 0= IF EXIT THEN
  REPEAT
; IMMEDIATE

: \
\ Comment till end of line.
  SOURCE >IN ! DROP ; IMMEDIATE

: >BODY ( xt --- a-addr)
\ Convert execution token to parameter field address.
  CELL+ ;

: (;CODE) ( --- )
\ Runtime for DOES>, exit calling definition and make last defined word
\ execute the calling definition after (;CODE)
  LAST @ NAME> R> OVER - CELLBYTES-TOK - SWAP ! ;

: DOES>  ( --- )
\ Word that contains DOES> will change the behavior of the last created
\ word such that it pushes its parameter field address onto the stack
\ and then executes whatever comes after DOES>
  ?COMP POSTPONE (;CODE) RFROM-TOK , \ Compile the R> primitive, which is the first
                        \ instruction that the defined word performs.
; IMMEDIATE

\ PART 10: TOP LEVEL OF INTERPRETER

: ?STACK ( ---)
\ Check for stack over/underflow and abort with an error if needed.
  DEPTH DUP 0< SWAP 10000 > OR ABORT" Stack error" ;

: INTERPRET ( ---)
\ Interpret words from the current source until the input source is exhausted.
\ The stack is checked where it can have changed: after a word executes and
\ after a number is interpreted. A word or a number compiled leaves it as it
\ was, so most of a source pays no check (the audit, Iteration 156).
  BEGIN
   32 WORD DUP C@
  WHILE
   FIND DUP
   IF
    -1 = STATE @ AND
    IF
     COMPILE,
    ELSE
     EXECUTE ?STACK
    THEN
   ELSE DROP
    NUMBER? DUP 0= HANDLER @ AND IF -13 THROW THEN 0= ABORT" Undefined word"
    \ A point made it a double (Forth 2012, 3.4.1.3): both cells kept, and
    \ compiled as two literals; a single drops its high cell (Iteration 167).
    DPL @ 0< IF DROP STATE @ IF LITERAL ELSE ?STACK THEN
    ELSE STATE @ IF SWAP LITERAL LITERAL ELSE ?STACK THEN THEN
   THEN
  REPEAT   DROP
;

: EVALUATE ( c-addr u --- )
\ Evaluate the string c-addr u as if it were typed on the terminal.
  SID @ >R SRC @ >R #SRC @ >R  >IN @ >R
  #SRC ! SRC ! 0 >IN ! -1 SID ! INTERPRET
  R> >IN ! R> #SRC ! R> SRC ! R> SID ! ;

VARIABLE INCLUDE-BUFFER ( --- a-addr)
\ This is the buffer where the lines of included files are stored.
508 ALLOT-T

VARIABLE INCLUDE-POINTER ( --- a-addr)
\ This variable holds the address where the included line is stored.

: INCLUDE-FILE ( fid --- )
\ Read lines from the file identified by fid and interpret them.
\ INCLUDE and EVALUATE nest in arbitrary order.
  INCLUDE-POINTER @ >R SID @ >R SRC @ >R #SRC @ >R >IN @ >R
  #SRC @ INCLUDE-POINTER +! INCLUDE-POINTER @ SRC !
  SID !
  BEGIN
   REFILL
  WHILE
   INTERPRET
  REPEAT
  R> >IN ! R> #SRC ! R> SRC ! R> SID ! R> INCLUDE-POINTER !
;

: INCLUDED  ( c-addr u ---- )
\ Open the file with name c-addr u and interpret all lines contained in it.
  R/O OPEN-FILE ABORT" Can't open include file"
  DUP >R INCLUDE-FILE
  R> CLOSE-FILE DROP
;

: QUIT ( --- )
\ This word resets the return stack, resets the compiler state, the include
\ buffer and then it reads and interprets terminal input.
  R0 @ RP! 0 HANDLER ! [
  TIB SRC ! 0 SID !
  INCLUDE-BUFFER INCLUDE-POINTER !
  BEGIN
   REFILL DROP INTERPRET STATE @ 0= IF ." OK" THEN CR
  0 UNTIL
;

: WARM ( ---)
\ This word is called when an error occurs. Clears the stacks, sets
\ BASE to decimal, closes the files and resets the search order.
  R0 @ RP! S0 @ SP! DECIMAL
  2 #ORDER !
  FORTH-WORDLIST CONTEXT !
  FORTH-WORDLIST CONTEXT CELL+ !
  FORTH-WORDLIST CURRENT !
  QUIT ;

: COLD ( --- )
\ The first word that is called at the start of Forth.
  START ! START @ DP +!
  \ Every thread head is stored as an offset from START and relocated
  \ here, which is what the single head used to need. Cell 0 is the
  \ thread COUNT and must not be touched; an empty thread is 0 and
  \ must stay 0, or it would become a pointer to the image base.
  FORTH-WORDLIST @ 1+ 1 DO
    FORTH-WORDLIST I CELLS + DUP @ ?DUP IF START @ + SWAP ! ELSE DROP THEN
  LOOP
  SP@ S0 !
  RP@ CELL+ R0 ! \ Initialize variables SO and RO
  BOOT @ ?DUP IF START @ + EXECUTE THEN
  \ A turnkey image's own boot word normally never returns (shell.4's
  \ MAIN either exits or runs its own read-eval loop). If one does
  \ return, fall through to the ordinary interactive behavior rather
  \ than doing something surprising.
  ." Welcome to Forth " CR
  WARM ;

END-CROSS
